      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "In a randomised, double-blind, double-dummy, multiple dose, crossover study in 30 patients we compared an ibuprofen/codeine combination (400 mg ibuprofen/25.6 mg codeine phosphate) with a paracetamol/codeine/caffeine combination (1 g paracetamol/16 mg codeine phosphate/60 mg caffeine) for pain relief over 6 days after two-stage bilateral lower third molar removal. The ibuprofen combination produced significantly greater analgesia than the paracetamol combination, both on single-dose analysis of the first and second days and on multiple-dose measures for days 1, 2, 3 and 4. The mean incidence of adverse effects over the 6 days was 20% for both combinations. This trial design (crossover with multiple dosing in outpatients) is a sensitive way of testing for analgesia, and is potentially more predictive of adverse effect problems than single-dose studies. It confirms that multiple dosing may show increased efficacy."},
